WEBVTT

00:00:00.538 --> 00:00:08.725
Engineers use design storms to estimate flooding and building damage. These rainfall scenarios have a specified duration and likelihood.

00:00:09.088 --> 00:00:19.012
In Louisiana’s Vermilion River Basin, we tested how the distribution of rainfall across the landscape changes which buildings flood and their estimated damage.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:28.175
Conventional flood modeling in the United States commonly uses NOAA Atlas 14 rainfall estimates, applied uniformly across a basin.

00:00:28.624 --> 00:00:37.225
Here, the Atlas 14 estimate for a 100-year, 24-hour storm is about 310 millimeters after adjustment for basin area.

00:00:37.674 --> 00:00:41.750
“100-year” means a one-percent chance of being exceeded in any year.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:46.812
Following common practice, we applied the same rainfall total everywhere.

00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:54.799
Yet real storms vary from place to place, making uniform rainfall over such a large area an unrealistic simplification.

00:00:56.146 --> 00:01:03.746
To represent rainfall variability, researchers have developed methods such as stochastic storm transposition, or SST.

00:01:04.146 --> 00:01:09.946
SST repositions observed storms within a region while preserving their spatial rainfall patterns.

00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:19.108
We simulated fifty SST storms for the same design duration and probability, exploring different placements of heavy rainfall across the basin.

00:01:20.458 --> 00:01:27.208
We then used the same hydrologic and hydraulic models to calculate runoff and flooding under both rainfall approaches.

00:01:27.596 --> 00:01:32.796
Around Lafayette and Scott, the blue maps show how maximum flood depths change.

00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:40.620
Depth-damage relationships translate water depth relative to each building’s first floor into a fraction of its value lost to flooding.

00:01:41.974 --> 00:01:53.024
To compare the full set of storms, SSTmax combines the greatest flood depth at each location across all fifty SST simulations. It does not describe a single event.

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:58.712
These maps show where flooded buildings cluster, with red marking stronger concentrations.

00:01:59.099 --> 00:02:06.486
Compared with Atlas 14, SSTmax produces stronger clusters around Lafayette, Scott, and Abbeville.

00:02:07.846 --> 00:02:16.408
Terrain helps explain these patterns. Height Above Nearest Drainage, or HAND, measures how high land sits above the channel it drains to.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:25.146
Here, higher ground borders much of the main river, while many neighborhoods along smaller tributaries occupy intermediate drainage heights.

00:02:26.469 --> 00:02:28.944
This chart groups flooded buildings by HAND.

00:02:29.344 --> 00:02:36.319
Gray bars show buildings flooded under Atlas 14; red bars show additional buildings flooded only under SST.

00:02:36.706 --> 00:02:40.732
Each curve shows the percentage of its group below a given drainage height.

00:02:41.132 --> 00:02:50.807
The highlighted ten-to-twenty-meter interval accounts for about sixty percent of the area between these curves. The exposure patterns differ most at these intermediate drainage heights.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:03:01.475
Across the fifty simulations, 11,518 buildings flooded at least once under SST but stayed dry under Atlas 14.

00:03:01.874 --> 00:03:12.287
For this additional group, potential structural losses totaled about 110 million dollars. These estimates combine simulated outcomes rather than describe one observed flood.

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:24.254
A uniform design storm can produce large basin-wide loss estimates while overlooking vulnerable neighborhoods. Representing spatial rainfall variability helps reveal those gaps.

00:03:24.654 --> 00:03:31.717
You can explore the rainfall, flooding, and exposure maps in our interactive companion using the link accompanying this video.
